\chapter{weft for AI and Introspection}
\label{ch:ai}

A literate web is a graph: output files are built from fragments,
fragments reference other fragments, and every line of tangled code came
from a known place in the source. weft exposes that graph through a small
set of flags designed for scripts, editors, and AI agents --- so a tool
can navigate a web precisely instead of reading it top to bottom. None of
these commands weave or tangle; they only report.

\section{The project map: \texttt{-m}}

\verb|weft -m project.weft| prints a JSON description of the whole web to
standard output: every output file and the fragments that compose it,
every fragment definition with its source location, every use
(cross-reference), and transitive dependencies. Pipe it through
\verb|jq| to answer structural questions:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -m project.weft | jq '.files["server.js"]'      # what builds this file?
weft -m project.weft | jq '.fragments["Validate input"]'  # where defined?
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

Two flags enrich the map. \verb|--bodies| adds each scrap's raw source
code; \verb|--prose| adds the paragraph of documentation that introduces
each fragment. Together they let a tool read a fragment's code \emph{and}
its rationale without opening the file:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -m --bodies --prose project.weft \
  | jq '.fragments["Validate input"].defs[0] | {prose, body}'
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{Extract one fragment: \texttt{-e}}

\verb|weft -e "Validate input" project.weft| emits just that fragment and
all the fragments it depends on, as a self-contained Markdown document ---
the exact code a tool needs for one task, with syntax highlighting and
source locations, and nothing else. The name matches with the same prefix
logic as \hbox{\tt @@<name...@@>}.

Add \verb|--callers| to see the other direction --- who uses this
fragment, transitively, as an indented tree from the \verb|@@o| roots
down:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -e "Validate input" --callers project.weft
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{Reverse map: \texttt{-R}}

Given a line in a \emph{tangled} file, \verb|-R| tells you where it came
from --- by reading the section markers already embedded in that file. No
\verb|.weft| sources are consulted, so it works in CI and on shipped
output:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -R server.js:47
  {"scrap": 3, "file": "routes.weft", "line": 42}

weft -R server.js            # every scrap region in the file
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{Lint the graph: \texttt{--lint}}

\verb|weft --lint project.weft| runs a post-parse check and reports
\emph{unused} fragments (defined but never referenced by any \verb|@@o| or
\verb|@@d|), \emph{undefined} references (used in a \hbox{\tt @@<name@@>}
but never defined), and, for an undefined reference with a close match, a
fuzzy suggestion of the name you probably meant.

\section{Preview and translate}

\verb|weft --diff project.weft| tangles to temporary files and shows a
unified diff of what \emph{would} change on disk --- a safe preview, and a
CI check that the committed output is up to date.

\verb|--errors| closes the loop with your compiler. Pipe a build log in,
and every \textit{file:line} it recognises is annotated with the matching
\verb|.weft| location (again, read from section markers --- no sources
needed):

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
make 2>&1 | weft --errors
  src/output.c:42: error: undeclared identifier
    [weft: literate/map-output.weft:77, scrap 204]
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\section{A directed workflow}

These commands compose into a fast loop for navigating a web you did not
write --- the pattern an AI agent (or a careful human) can follow:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Orient} --- \verb|weft --help|, then
  \verb|weft -m --prose| for the structure and rationale.
\item \textbf{Locate} --- find the fragment relevant to the task in the map.
\item \textbf{Extract} --- \verb|weft -e "name"| for exactly that code;
  \verb|--callers| to see its impact.
\item \textbf{Edit} --- change the \verb|.weft| source at the reported
  location (never the tangled output).
\item \textbf{Preview} --- \verb|weft --diff| to see what tangling changes.
\item \textbf{Verify} --- re-tangle, build, and pipe errors through
  \verb|weft --errors| to map any failure back to source.
\item \textbf{Check} --- \verb|weft --lint| for orphans and typos.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent The map and reverse map are complementary: \verb|-m| goes from
source to structure, \verb|-R|/\verb|--errors| go from tangled output
back to source. A tool never has to guess.
